#! s137's calls in the one surface an editor user reads a type from (le08).
#!
#! le07 wrote `requests/hover-byte` because v0.2.4 moved a TYPE
#! (`str.bytes()`: `List[int]` -> `List[byte]`) and a header-only sweep
#! proves the wire did not move while proving nothing about a type the
#! library never bound. v0.2.5 is the opposite pin and this transcript is
#! its counterpart: s137 adds FIVE builtin FUNCTIONS
#! (`net_listen_with`, `net_wait`, `os_spawn_with`, `net_adopt_listener`,
#! `os_cpus`) and NO type — `BUILTIN_TYPES` is byte-identical across the
#! pins at seventeen names, and what grew is `PRELUDE`.
#!
#! So the prediction is that hover does not move, and this file is the
#! measurement that makes the prediction falsifiable rather than an
#! assumption. `net_wait` is the s137 call worth hovering: alone among
#! the five its signature is interesting at both ends
#! (`List[int] -> List[int]`), so a server that had learned anything at
#! all about s137's names would have to show it here.
#!
#! Five positions on `net/wait_readiness.lu`, s137's own witness:
#!   41:8   `let woke = net_wait(set, 5000)?` — the binding taking the
#!                                     ANSWER. `List[int]`: the subset,
#!                                     in the caller's order.
#!   41:15  `net_wait` itself           — hover on the builtin FUNCTION
#!                                     NAME, and the load-bearing line.
#!                                     It does NOT answer `null`, which
#!                                     is what hover on the builtin TYPE
#!                                     name `byte` answers (`hover-byte`
#!                                     15:16) and what le08 predicted
#!                                     here by analogy. Measured, it
#!                                     answers
#!
#!                                         List[int] ! {io}
#!
#!                                     over the range 15..34 — the whole
#!                                     CALL expression `net_wait(set,
#!                                     5000)`, not the name. So the two
#!                                     builtin namespaces are asymmetric
#!                                     in the editor: a builtin TYPE name
#!                                     hovers to nothing, a builtin
#!                                     FUNCTION name hovers to the type
#!                                     of the call it heads. That is the
#!                                     prediction-by-analogy failing, and
#!                                     the reason this position is in the
#!                                     script rather than reasoned about.
#!
#!                                     Note what it is NOT: a signature.
#!                                     No parameter names, no arity, no
#!                                     clause prose — hovering the name
#!                                     of a call the language gained on
#!                                     this very pin tells a reader only
#!                                     what the expression evaluates to.
#!                                     Note also what it IS: the error
#!                                     row rides the string, and
#!                                     `List[int] ! {io}` is character
#!                                     for character the signature tail
#!                                     `[os.net.wait]` writes for this
#!                                     call. The editor prints the
#!                                     clause's own words without having
#!                                     been taught them.
#!   34:8   `let set = List[int]()`     — the ARGUMENT. Same type as the
#!                                     answer, so both ends of the call
#!                                     are pinned by one string.
#!   38:8   `let quiet_is_empty = net_wait(set, 20)?.len == 0`
#!                                     — `bool`, through `?` immediately
#!                                     followed by a field access and
#!                                     then a comparison. The chain the
#!                                     clause's "an EMPTY answer is an
#!                                     answer, not a failure" sentence
#!                                     is about.
#!   31:8   `let a = net_listen(...)`   — a net HANDLE is an `int`. Pins
#!                                     that `[os.net.listen.opts]`'s
#!                                     "acquisition call and nothing
#!                                     else" rule reaches the editor as
#!                                     the plain integer it is, and not
#!                                     as some opaque handle type.
name      requests/hover-net-wait
profile   minimal

initialize
open      net/wait_readiness.lu
wait      diagnostics net/wait_readiness.lu
req       hover net/wait_readiness.lu 41:8
req       hover net/wait_readiness.lu 41:15
req       hover net/wait_readiness.lu 34:8
req       hover net/wait_readiness.lu 38:8
req       hover net/wait_readiness.lu 31:8
shutdown
